    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The subgrade, the layer beneath the asphalt overlay, plays a significant role in the overall performance of the pavement. In the case of premature longitudinal cracking, the subgrade can be a contributing factor, especially if it's not properly compacted or if it's prone to moisture issues.\n\nCompaction of the subgrade is critical to ensure that the pavement has a stable base. If the subgrade is not compacted adequately, it can lead to settlement and unevenness, which can cause the asphalt overlay to crack. A study on subgrade compaction found that a minimum of 95% of the maximum density of the subgrade material is required to prevent settlement and cracking.\n\nMoisture levels in the subgrade can also exacerbate cracking. If the subgrade is too wet or too dry, it can lead to thermal expansion and contraction of the pavement, causing cracks to form. In the case of Phoenix, the high desert temperatures and low humidity can lead to rapid evaporation of moisture from the subgrade, causing it to become dry and brittle. This can lead to thermal expansion and contraction of the pavement, causing cracks to form.\n\nWhen examining the soil profile, you should look for indicators of subgrade compaction and moisture issues. Some common",
